    "updated_at": "2021-01-23T09:15:57Z",
    "body": "IMHO, in your suggestion there is redundant information, which would make implementation a bit complex.\r\n\r\nAnd I'm talking just about a full \"prompt\" line here, and not about the actual input/command line (as other rules apply there). \r\n\r\nThe line above the prompt has a maximum number of columns, which could be retrieved by _Starship_. Therefore \"right aligning\" should be easily doable by calculating/counting the chars which will be rendered and subtracting that from the width of the terminal. Therefore, IMHO, you would need only one additional module. Let me elaborate by providing an example:\r\n\r\n```toml\r\nformat = \"\"\"\r\n$username$hostname$directory $filler $time\r\n$character\r\n\"\"\"\r\n\r\n[filler]\r\nwidth = \"full\" ### could be either \"full\" or a number. Any number would mean, repeat it number-times and \"full\" would fill to the amount of the terminal width (see above)\r\nformat = \"[-=-](fg:gray)\"\r\n```\r\n\r\nThe output would look like\r\n\r\n```\r\njefferson in airplane in ~/git/starship -=--=--=--=--=--=--=--=- at 23:42\r\n❯ \r\n```\r\n\r\nThat should give the most flexibility for customization, while keeping the implementation as simple as possible.\r\n\r\nFor reference if you want to use left/right prompt on the actual input/command line, please take a look a the discussion _Aligning modules to the right side of the shell._ #2046",
